Here's the code:

```java
class Solution {
    public boolean isPowerOfThree(int n) {
        // Rule out negative numbers and zeros
        if (n <= 0 || (n & -n)!= n) return false;

        // Check if the number has more than one bit set
        int bitsSet = 0;
        while ((n >>>= 1) > 0) {
            bitsSet += n & 1;
            if (bitsSet > 1) return false;
        }

        // Make sure that the last digit before the final non-zero digit is 0
        return (n & 1) == 0 && (n & (n >> Integer.numberOfTrailingZeros(n))) == 0;
    }
}
```

First, we use the bitwise AND operator (`&`) to ensure that only positive values remain.

Then we use a loop to count the number of bits set until the value goes down to zero. We stop checking as soon as the leading bit changes from 1 to 0 because it guarantees that the number of trailing 0s equals the exponent.

Finally, we use another bitwise mask combined with `numberOfTrailingZeros` function provided by the `Integer` class to ensure that the number does not have any trailing bits set. If there aren't any trailing 1s, the condition `(n & (n >> Integer.numberOfTrailingZeros(n))) == 0` holds and returns true. Otherwise, it fails and returns false.

